INSERT OR IGNORE INTO skills (id, slug, name, one_line, category, sub, for_tag, works_in, skill_md, examples, example_inputs, maker_id, version, status, test, test_skill, test_plain, created_at, updated_at, live_at) VALUES ('polish-email', 'polish-email', 'Polish my email', 'Your rough draft, shorter and clearer, still you.', 'writing', 'fix-writing', 'anyone', '["claude-ai","claude-code"]', '---
name: polish-email
description: Clean up a rough email, text or work message so it is shorter and clearer and still sounds like the person who wrote it. Returns a subject line, the polished message, a short list of what changed, anything risky to check before sending, and warmer or firmer versions of the key line. Use when someone pastes a draft and says things like "polish this email", "make this sound better", "fix my email", "does this sound rude", "make this more professional", "clean this up before I send it", "help me reply to this", or "rewrite my message". Not for writing a brand new email from nothing with no draft.
---

# Polish my email

The person wrote a draft. Your job is to make it better and still theirs. Plain AI rewrites tend to fail in the same ways: they make the email longer, add pleasantries the person never says, swap their words for stiff office words, bury the ask, and quietly add facts. Do the opposite.

## Before you start

Work with what you have. Do not ask questions if there is a draft. Assume:
- The goal is the main thing the draft asks for or tells.
- The reader is whoever the draft is addressed to.
- The tone should stay close to theirs, only clearer and calmer.

Ask at most one short question only if the draft is so unclear you cannot tell what it asks for. If there is no draft at all, ask them to paste it.

## How to edit

1. **Find the point.** Write it to yourself in one sentence: what does the reader need to know or do? Put that near the top of the email, in the first two sentences.
2. **One clear ask.** If they want something, say it once, plainly, as a request: who does what by when. Use the deadline they gave. If they gave none, write `by [day]` and flag it. Never pick a date for them.
3. **Cut.** Remove repeats, long apologies, backstory the reader already knows, and "just wanted to" filler. The result should be shorter than the draft, usually by a quarter to a half. The only exception is adding the missing ask.
4. **Keep their voice.** Keep their greeting and sign-off style (if they write "Hi Sam," and "Thanks, Jo", keep that). Keep contractions, their key words and phrases, their level of formality, and any detail only they would know. Fix spelling, grammar and punctuation. Swap a word only if it is wrong, unclear or could offend.
5. **Calm the heat.** Keep firmness. Remove blame, sarcasm, ALL CAPS, piles of "!!!" or "??", and guilt trips. State facts and the ask instead.
6. **Short paragraphs.** One to three sentences each. Use a short list only if there are three or more separate items (dates, steps, questions).
7. **Subject line.** For emails, write a plain subject of 3 to 8 words that says what it is about and, if there is a deadline, includes it. Skip the subject for texts and chat messages.

## Never

- Never invent facts, names, numbers, dates, prices, reasons or promises. If something is missing, use a `[bracket]` and flag it.
- Never add "I hope this email finds you well", "I hope you''re doing well", "Please don''t hesitate to reach out", "I wanted to touch base", "Thank you in advance", "Best regards" (unless they used it), or any line they would not say out loud.
- Never use these words unless the draft did: delve, leverage, ensure, utilize, align, seamless, robust, kindly, circle back, at your earliest convenience, moving forward, valued.
- No em dashes. Use a period or a comma.
- No exclamation marks beyond what they used. One is fine if they used many.
- Do not change who is speaking, what they agreed to, or how much they are asking for.
- Do not lecture them about their tone. Flag it in one line and fix it.

## What to check for risk

Look for these and list only the ones that apply:
- Blame, sarcasm or anger the reader could forward to someone else.
- A mention of "attached", "see below" or a link, so they remember to attach or paste it.
- Missing deadline, amount, date or name you had to bracket.
- A promise or admission they may not want in writing (for example "it was my fault", "I''ll refund you", "I can do it for free").
- Private details about someone else, or a complaint about a coworker sent to many people.
- A name that is spelled two different ways in the draft.

## Output format

Use exactly this layout. Keep everything after the email short.

```
**Subject:** <subject line>   (emails only)

<the polished message, ready to copy>

---
**What I changed**
- <3 to 5 bullets, each under 12 words, plain: "Moved your ask to the top", "Cut the second apology", "Added a deadline slot">

**Check before you send**
- <only real risks from the list above; write "Nothing risky I can see." if none>

**Want a different tone?** Swap in one of these for <name the line, e.g. "your ask">:
- Warmer: "<one rewritten sentence>"
- Firmer: "<one rewritten sentence>"

<draft word count> words → <new word count> words
```

The main version is the "as-is but clean" tone. Only give the warmer and firmer options as a single swapped sentence, not whole new emails. If they ask for a full warmer or firmer version afterward, write it with the same rules.

## Self-check before you answer

Read the polished email once more and fix anything that fails:
- Is it shorter than the draft (or only longer because of a needed ask)?
- Would the person recognize it as theirs? Their greeting, sign-off and key words are still there.
- Is the ask in the first two sentences, said once, with a deadline or a `[day]` slot?
- Every fact, name, date and number appears in the draft. Nothing new.
- No banned phrases, no em dashes, no new pleasantries.
- The word count line is correct.
', '[{"input":"can you make this sound better before i send it? i dont want to sound rude but i really need to get paid. its to a client\n\nhey Marcus,\n\nso I''m just following up again about the invoice for the Brightwell Bakery logo and menu stuff, I sent it on Sept 30 and it was due Oct 30 and its now almost 4 weeks past that. I know you guys are busy and I totally get it, I''m sorry to keep bugging you about this. The total is $1,850 and I''ve attached it again. Also just so you know the menu changes you asked for last week (the extra gluten free page and redoing the prices) weren''t part of the original quote so that would be extra, probably like $200, I didn''t say anything at the time cause I didn''t want to be difficult but I should have. Honestly I''ve had to chase this 3 times now and it''s kind of frustrating because I delivered everything on time?? I''d really appreciate if you could pay it this week. Also I can''t start the window signs until the invoice is sorted. Sorry again and thanks so much!!\n\nTalia","output":"**Subject:** Overdue invoice, Brightwell Bakery, $1,850\n\nHey Marcus,\n\nI''m following up on the Brightwell Bakery logo and menu invoice. I sent it Sept 30, it was due Oct 30, and it''s now almost 4 weeks overdue. Could you pay the $1,850 this week? I''ve attached it again.\n\nI can''t start the window signs until it''s sorted.\n\nThe menu changes you asked for last week (the extra gluten free page and redone prices) weren''t in the original quote, so they''ll be extra, probably around $200. I should have said so at the time.\n\nThanks,\nTalia\n\n---\n**What I changed**\n- Moved the ask and the amount to the top\n- Cut the apologies and the \"I know you''re busy\" line\n- Dropped the frustration and the \"chased 3 times\" line\n- Grouped the window signs and extra $200 into their own short paragraphs\n\n**Check before you send**\n- Attach the invoice, since the email says it''s attached.\n- The extra $200 is an estimate (\"probably around\"). Confirm the number if you can.\n- \"I should have said so at the time\" is an admission in writing. Cut it if you''d rather not.\n- You gave \"this week\" as the deadline. Swap in a day, like \"by Friday\", if you want it firmer.\n\n**Want a different tone?** Swap in one of these for your ask:\n- Warmer: \"Could you pay the $1,850 this week if you get a chance? I''ve attached it again.\"\n- Firmer: \"Please pay the $1,850 by [day this week]. I''ve attached it again.\"\n\n170 words → 87 words","plain_output":"Here''s a polished version. It stays friendly but is clear and firm about payment.\n\n**Subject: Payment due: Brightwell Bakery invoice ($1,850)**\n\nHi Marcus,\n\nI''m following up on the invoice for the Brightwell Bakery logo and menu design. I sent it on September 30, and payment was due October 30. It''s now almost four weeks overdue. I''ve attached it again for convenience.\n\n- **Amount due:** $1,850\n- **Original due date:** October 30\n\nCould you please arrange payment by the end of this week? I''ll also need the invoice settled before I can start on the window signs.\n\nSeparately, the menu changes you requested last week (the extra gluten-free page and the updated prices) were outside the original quote. I should have said so at the time. I''ll send a separate invoice of about $200 for that work. Let me know if you''d like to talk it over first.\n\nI''ve enjoyed working on this project and look forward to finishing the window signs. Thanks for your help getting this sorted.\n\nBest,\nTalia\n\n---\n\n**What I changed and why**\n\n- **Cut the apologies.** \"Sorry to keep bugging you\" and \"sorry again\" made the request sound like a favor. You''re asking for payment you''re owed, and you can do that politely without apologizing.\n- **Removed the frustration.** \"Chase this 3 times\" and \"I delivered everything on time??\" are understandable, but they''d put Marcus on the defensive. The facts (dates, amount, how overdue) make the point without them.\n- **Gave a clear deadline.** \"This week\" is now a specific request with a reason, which is the window signs.\n- **Separated the extra $200.** Mixed in with the overdue invoice, it could give Marcus an excuse to delay the whole payment. It''s now its own short paragraph, and the original invoice stays the priority.\n- **Added a subject line** so the invoice is easy to find.\n\n**Two things to consider**\n\n1. If the $200 is a firm number, say \"$200\" rather than \"about $200\". Vague amounts invite haggling. Better still, send the extra work as its own invoice with a short breakdown.\n2. If you''ve already chased this several times, you could add a line such as \"Please let me know if there''s anything holding up payment.\" It gives him an easy way to explain a delay. It also leaves a written record if you have to escalate, for example with a late fee, if your contract allows one.\n\n**Shorter, firmer option** (if the first version feels too soft):\n\n> Hi Marcus, invoice #[number] for $1,850 (Brightwell Bakery logo and menu design) was due October 30 and is now almost four weeks overdue. It''s attached again. Please pay by Friday. I can''t start the window signs until it''s settled. The recent menu changes (extra gluten-free page, revised prices) were outside the original quote, so I''ll invoice them separately, about $200. Thanks, Talia","plain_source":"test"}]', 'can you make this sound better before i send it? i dont want to sound rude but i really need to get paid. its to a client

hey Marcus,

so I''m just following up again about the invoice for the Brightwell Bakery logo and menu stuff, I sent it on Sept 30 and it was due Oct 30 and its now almost 4 weeks past that. I know you guys are busy and I totally get it, I''m sorry to keep bugging you about this. The total is $1,850 and I''ve attached it again. Also just so you know the menu changes you asked for last week (the extra gluten free page and redoing the prices) weren''t part of the original quote so that would be extra, probably like $200, I didn''t say anything at the time cause I didn''t want to be difficult but I should have. Honestly I''ve had to chase this 3 times now and it''s kind of frustrating because I delivered everything on time?? I''d really appreciate if you could pay it this week. Also I can''t start the window signs until the invoice is sorted. Sorry again and thanks so much!!

Talia', 'smez-system', 1, 'live', '{"skill_score":4.8,"plain_score":3.5,"winner":"skill","reason":"the skill is a tight, ready-to-send email in Talia''s voice that adds no new facts and flags the risky lines, while plain Claude invents a commitment to send a separate invoice and a line about enjoying the project, and pads with extra options.","tested_at":"2026-11-23"}', 4.8, 3.5, 1795366800000, 1795366800000, 1795366800000);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO skills (id, slug, name, one_line, category, sub, for_tag, works_in, skill_md, examples, example_inputs, maker_id, version, status, test, test_skill, test_plain, created_at, updated_at, live_at) VALUES ('study-buddy', 'study-buddy', 'Study buddy', 'Your notes become a quiz you can play.', 'study', 'study-tools', 'student', '["claude-ai","claude-code"]', '---
name: study-buddy
description: Turns someone''s study notes (or a topic) into a playable quiz and flashcard app in one HTML file. Questions test understanding with mixed formats, explain every answer, and bring missed questions back until they stick. Use when someone says "quiz me on my notes", "make flashcards from this", "help me study", "turn my notes into a quiz", "make a practice test", "I have an exam on...", or pastes class notes and wants to revise.
---

# Study buddy

You build one HTML file: a quiz and flashcard app made from the user''s notes. A tested starter app is in `assets/study-buddy.html`. Your job is the questions. The app already does the rest (mixed formats, feedback, missed ones come back, simple spaced repetition across visits, score, finish screen, sound toggle, keyboard keys, saving with a fallback when storage is blocked).

## 1. Get the material (ask at most 2 quick questions)

- **Notes pasted:** start building. Don''t ask anything.
- **Only a topic** (for example "the French Revolution"): ask once, "Can you paste your notes or the page you''re studying? Then every question matches what your teacher covers." If they say no or just want to go, write a short notes summary (10 to 20 plain bullet points, the level they mention or high school if unsure), show it to them first, and build only from that summary.
- If they mention a test date, a level, or a focus, use it. Don''t ask for these.

## 2. Write the questions

Read the notes twice. Make a list of the facts and ideas the notes actually state. Then write:

- **Questions:** 1 per idea worth knowing, usually 10 to 15 (fewer for short notes, never more than 20). Mix the formats: about half multiple choice, a quarter fill in, a quarter true or false.
- **Flashcards:** 6 to 15 key terms, names or dates with a short back (under 20 words).

Make questions test understanding, not just memory. In every set include:
- at least 3 "why" or "what causes" questions,
- at least 2 "what would happen if" or "which example fits" questions that apply an idea to a new case,
- at least 1 question that compares or orders two things from the notes.
Plain recall ("In what year...") is fine for at most a third of the set.

Format rules:
- **Multiple choice:** 4 options, one clearly right. Wrong options are real mix-ups a student would make (a nearby date, the other person, the cause swapped with the effect), never jokes or "all of the above". Keep all 4 about the same length so the answer doesn''t stand out.
- **Fill in:** the blank is one word or a short name. Put `___` where it goes. Add common spellings and short forms to `accept` (for example "Robespierre", "Maximilien Robespierre").
- **True or false:** the false ones change one real detail; the `why` says what is actually true.
- **`why`:** one or two short sentences that teach, written to a friend. Not "Correct!" and not a repeat of the question.
- **`from`:** a short quote or close paraphrase from the notes (under 20 words) that backs the answer.

## 3. Hard rules about facts

- Every fact comes only from the notes (or the summary they saw). Don''t add outside facts, even true ones.
- If a note is unclear, contradicts itself, or could be read two ways, skip it. Don''t guess.
- If the notes have a clear mistake, don''t quiz it. Mention it in one line in your reply.
- No trick questions. No questions about the notes themselves ("What is the third bullet?").

## 4. Build the file

1. Open `assets/study-buddy.html`. Copy it exactly.
2. Replace only the JSON inside `<script id="deck" type="application/json">`. Keep every other line as it is.
3. Fill it like this (valid JSON: double quotes, no trailing commas, escape any `"` inside text as `\"`):

```json
{
  "title": "The French Revolution",
  "subtitle": "From your history notes",
  "questions": [
    {"type": "mc", "q": "...", "options": ["...", "...", "...", "..."], "answer": "exact text of the right option", "why": "...", "from": "..."},
    {"type": "fill", "q": "The ___ was stormed on 14 July 1789.", "answer": "Bastille", "accept": ["the Bastille"], "why": "...", "from": "..."},
    {"type": "tf", "q": "a statement", "answer": false, "why": "...", "from": "..."}
  ],
  "cards": [{"front": "Estates-General", "back": "..."}]
}
```

- `title`: the topic in 1 to 4 words. `subtitle`: "From your ___ notes".
- `answer` for multiple choice must match one option exactly, character for character.
- Don''t number the questions; the app shuffles them.
- Output the whole file as one HTML artifact (or a file named `<topic>-study-buddy.html` if you can create files). Never send only the JSON or a "rest stays the same" snippet.

If the starter file can''t be read, build the same app from scratch: same features, one file, no outside scripts, a Google Fonts link at most.

## 5. Check before you answer

- Every `answer` in multiple choice is one of its `options`. Every fill-in `q` has `___`. True or false `answer` is `true` or `false`, not a string.
- Count the mix: understanding questions are at least two thirds.
- Pick 3 questions at random and find the fact in the notes. If you can''t, delete the question.
- The JSON parses (no stray quotes, no comments, no trailing commas).
- Picture it at 375 px wide: long options wrap, no word is wider than the screen (break very long terms with a space or a hyphen if needed).
- Nothing outside the deck block changed. Storage code still sits inside try/catch.

## 6. Your reply

Keep it short:
- One line: what''s in it (for example "14 questions and 10 flashcards from your notes").
- One line on how to use it: "Press Quiz me. Keys 1 to 4 pick an answer, T or F for true or false, Enter for next. Anything you miss comes back."
- If you skipped unclear notes or spotted a mistake, say which in one line.
No long recap, no list of every question.
', '[{"input":"can you make me a quiz from my notes? science test on friday\n\nWater cycle notes, Year 8 science\n\n- Water moves between the sea, the air and the land in a loop called the water cycle. The sun powers it.\n- Evaporation: the sun heats water in seas, lakes and rivers and it turns into water vapour (a gas) that rises.\n- Transpiration: plants lose water vapour through tiny holes in their leaves. Evaporation + transpiration together = evapotranspiration.\n- Condensation: as water vapour rises the air gets colder, so the vapour cools and turns back into tiny liquid droplets. Lots of droplets make clouds.\n- Precipitation: when droplets join and get too heavy they fall as rain, snow, sleet or hail.\n- Collection: water that falls collects in oceans, lakes and rivers. Some runs over the ground (surface runoff) into rivers.\n- Infiltration: some water soaks into the ground and becomes groundwater. It can stay there a long time and slowly flows back to rivers and the sea.\n- About 97% of Earth''s water is salty sea water. Only about 3% is fresh water and most of that is frozen in ice caps and glaciers.\n- Warmer temperatures mean more evaporation.\n- Trees cut down = less transpiration, so the air above can get drier. (check this w/ Mr Patel)\n- The total amount of water on Earth stays about the same, it just changes state and place.\n- Key words: state change, water vapour, runoff, groundwater, reservoir (a store of water like a lake or an ice cap)","output":"The skill''s answer is a full web page. Open it: /projects/skill-quest/templates/study-buddy/result-skill.html","plain_output":"# Water Cycle Quiz (Year 8 Science)\n\nTry it without looking at your notes first. Answers are at the bottom.\n\n## Part A: Multiple choice\n\n**1.** What powers the water cycle?\nA) The wind\nB) The sun\nC) The moon\nD) Ocean currents\n\n**2.** What is water vapour?\nA) Water as a liquid\nB) Water as a solid\nC) Water as a gas\nD) Salty water\n\n**3.** What is the name for plants losing water vapour through tiny holes in their leaves?\nA) Condensation\nB) Infiltration\nC) Transpiration\nD) Collection\n\n**4.** Why does water vapour turn into droplets as it rises?\nA) The air gets warmer\nB) The air gets colder\nC) The sun gets stronger\nD) The wind pushes it down\n\n**5.** Which of these is NOT a form of precipitation?\nA) Sleet\nB) Hail\nC) Snow\nD) Water vapour\n\n**6.** What do we call water that soaks into the ground and stays there for a long time?\nA) Surface runoff\nB) Groundwater\nC) A reservoir\nD) Cloud water\n\n**7.** About what percentage of Earth''s water is fresh water?\nA) 3%\nB) 25%\nC) 50%\nD) 97%\n\n**8.** Where is most of Earth''s fresh water found?\nA) Rivers\nB) Lakes\nC) Frozen in ice caps and glaciers\nD) In clouds\n\n## Part B: True or false\n\n**9.** Warmer temperatures mean more evaporation.\n\n**10.** The total amount of water on Earth keeps getting smaller as it is used up.\n\n**11.** Surface runoff is water that runs over the ground into rivers.\n\n**12.** Condensation is when liquid water turns into a gas.\n\n## Part C: Fill in the blank\n\n**13.** Evaporation + transpiration together = ______________.\n\n**14.** Lots of tiny droplets together make ______________.\n\n**15.** A store of water such as a lake or an ice cap is called a ______________.\n\n## Part D: Short answer\n\n**16.** Put these in the correct order: precipitation, evaporation, condensation, collection.\n\n**17.** Explain what happens to water in the water cycle when it evaporates from the sea. Use the words *sun*, *water vapour* and *state change*.\n\n**18.** Cutting down lots of trees may make the air above drier. Explain why, using the word *transpiration*.\n\n**19.** Describe two different things that can happen to water after it falls as rain.\n\n---\n\n## Answers\n\n1. B\n2. C\n3. C\n4. B\n5. D\n6. B\n7. A\n8. C\n9. True\n10. False. The total amount stays about the same. It just changes state and place.\n11. True\n12. False. That is evaporation. Condensation is a gas (vapour) turning into liquid droplets.\n13. Evapotranspiration\n14. Clouds\n15. Reservoir\n16. Evaporation, condensation, precipitation, collection\n17. The sun heats the sea water, causing a state change from liquid to water vapour (a gas), which then rises.\n18. Trees release water vapour through transpiration. With fewer trees there is less transpiration, so less water vapour goes into the air and it can get drier.\n19. Any two: it collects in oceans, lakes and rivers; it runs over the ground as surface runoff into rivers; it soaks into the ground (infiltration) and becomes groundwater.\n\n---\n\n## Tips for Friday\n\n- **Check the tree note with Mr Patel.** Your notes say \"check this w/ Mr Patel\" next to the trees and transpiration point, so question 18 follows your notes but isn''t confirmed. Ask him before the test.\n- Cover the answers and mark yourself. Re-read the notes for any you missed.\n- The key words (state change, water vapour, runoff, groundwater, reservoir) are likely to come up, so make sure you can explain each in your own words.\n- Try drawing the water cycle from memory and labelling each stage.\n\nI can make a harder version, a flashcard set, or a quiz on just the ones you got wrong if you''d like. Good luck on Friday!","plain_source":"test","output_link":"/projects/skill-quest/templates/study-buddy/result-skill.html"}]', 'can you make me a quiz from my notes? science test on friday

Water cycle notes, Year 8 science

- Water moves between the sea, the air and the land in a loop called the water cycle. The sun powers it.
- Evaporation: the sun heats water in seas, lakes and rivers and it turns into water vapour (a gas) that rises.
- Transpiration: plants lose water vapour through tiny holes in their leaves. Evaporation + transpiration together = evapotranspiration.
- Condensation: as water vapour rises the air gets colder, so the vapour cools and turns back into tiny liquid droplets. Lots of droplets make clouds.
- Precipitation: when droplets join and get too heavy they fall as rain, snow, sleet or hail.
- Collection: water that falls collects in oceans, lakes and rivers. Some runs over the ground (surface runoff) into rivers.
- Infiltration: some water soaks into the ground and becomes groundwater. It can stay there a long time and slowly flows back to rivers and the sea.
- About 97% of Earth''s water is salty sea water. Only about 3% is fresh water and most of that is frozen in ice caps and glaciers.
- Warmer temperatures mean more evaporation.
- Trees cut down = less transpiration, so the air above can get drier. (check this w/ Mr Patel)
- The total amount of water on Earth stays about the same, it just changes state and place.
- Key words: state change, water vapour, runoff, groundwater, reservoir (a store of water like a lake or an ice cap)', 'smez-system', 1, 'live', '{"skill_score":4.5,"plain_score":4,"winner":"skill","reason":"the skill gives instant per-question feedback quoting the student''s own notes, retries misses, and skips the unverified Mr Patel note; plain Claude is accurate and includes short-answer questions and a helpful Mr Patel flag, but it is static, so the student has to mark themselves.","tested_at":"2026-11-23"}', 4.5, 4, 1795366800000, 1795366800000, 1795366800000);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO skills (id, slug, name, one_line, category, sub, for_tag, works_in, skill_md, examples, example_inputs, maker_id, version, status, test, test_skill, test_plain, created_at, updated_at, live_at) VALUES ('habit-tracker', 'habit-tracker', 'Habit tracker', 'Tap once a day. Watch your streak grow.', 'health', 'habits', 'anyone', '["claude-ai","claude-code"]', '---
name: habit-tracker
description: Builds a working habit tracker app in one HTML file, with one-tap check-ins, forgiving streaks (one missed day is covered by a "return ticket"), a month calendar, gentle stats, edit and delete, and backup to a file. Use when someone says "make me a habit tracker", "track my habits", "I want to drink more water / read more / work out every day", "streak tracker", "daily checklist app", "help me build a routine", or lists things they want to do every day.
---

# Habit tracker

You build one HTML file: a calm habit tracker set up with the user''s habits. A tested starter app is in `assets/habit-tracker.html`. Your job is to set it up well. The app already does the rest: one-tap check-ins, streaks with a return ticket, the month grid, stats, add/edit/delete, color picker, export and import, saving with a warning when the browser blocks storage, sound and a tiny buzz on check-in, dark mode, keyboard and screen reader support.

## 1. Read what they want (ask nothing unless you must)

- **They named habits:** build right away. Don''t ask questions.
- **They named nothing** ("make me a habit tracker"): build right away with the 3 sample habits already in the starter. Say they can delete them.
- **Only ask** (one short question) if a habit is so vague you can''t name it, for example "be better". Even then, offer a guess: "Want me to use ''Write 3 things I''m grateful for''?"

## 2. Turn their words into good habit names

Each habit is a short action you can tick off today. Rules:
- Start with a verb. Keep it under 30 characters. Sentence case.
- Keep their numbers: "read 20 minutes" stays "Read for 20 minutes".
- If they gave no amount, add a small, doable one only when the habit needs it to be checkable: "drink more water" becomes "Drink 8 glasses of water", "exercise" becomes "Move for 20 minutes". "Stretch" can stay "Stretch".
- One habit per idea. Split "read and journal" into two.
- Up to 12 habits. If they list more than 6, build them all, and suggest in one line that starting with 3 works better.
- Habits that aren''t daily ("gym 3 times a week") are still daily check-ins here. Mention in one line that the return ticket covers rest days.

## 3. Fill the setup block

1. Open `assets/habit-tracker.html`. Copy it exactly.
2. Replace only the JSON inside `<script id="setup" type="application/json">`. Keep every other line.
3. Fill it (valid JSON, double quotes, no trailing commas):

```json
{
  "title": "My habits",
  "color": "tide",
  "sample": false,
  "habits": [
    {"name": "Drink 8 glasses of water", "color": "tide"},
    {"name": "Read for 20 minutes", "color": "ochre"}
  ]
}
```

- `title`: "My habits", or "Sam''s habits" if they gave a name, or their own title if they asked for one. Max 32 characters.
- `color` (their personal color) and each habit `color` must be one of: `moss`, `tide`, `clay`, `ochre`, `rose`, `slate`. If they mention a favorite color, map it (green to moss, blue to tide, orange or red to clay, yellow to ochre, pink to rose, purple or grey to slate). Otherwise pick a color that fits each habit (water = tide, reading = ochre, outdoors or plants = moss, body or movement = clay, sleep = slate, kindness or people = rose) and give every habit a different color when you can.
- `sample`: `false` when the habits came from them, `true` only when you kept the starter''s samples.
- Don''t invent past check-ins. Every habit starts fresh today.

Output the whole file as one HTML artifact (or a file named `habit-tracker.html` if you can create files). Never send only the JSON or a snippet that says "rest stays the same".

If the starter file can''t be read, build the same app from scratch with the same features and rules below.

## 4. Hard rules (if you change or rebuild anything)

- One file. All CSS and JS inline. No outside scripts, images or network calls. A Google Fonts link is the only allowed outside file.
- Every `localStorage` read and write sits in try/catch. If storage is blocked, the app still works and shows the yellow "isn''t saving" note.
- Streak rule stays exactly: 1 return ticket at most; it covers one missed day; 7 check-ins after using it earn it back; two misses in a row end the streak; today not done yet never breaks a streak.
- No guilt words: never "failed", "broke", "lost". Missed days are just empty.
- Real buttons with labels, visible focus, text contrast at least 4.5:1, works at 375 px wide, honors reduced motion.
- No purple gradients, glow, glass effects, or emoji as icons.

## 5. Check before you answer

- The setup JSON parses, every color is one of the six names, every name is under 40 characters.
- Picture it at 375 px: each habit name fits on two lines at most.
- Tapping a habit checks it, tapping again unchecks it; the dots menu opens edit with delete; Settings has export, copy, import and start over.
- Nothing outside the setup block changed (unless you had to rebuild).

## 6. Your reply

Short and friendly:
- One line: what you set up ("Your tracker with water, reading and stretching").
- How it works in 3 short lines: tap a habit to check it off; miss one day and your return ticket saves the streak; tap a past day on the calendar if you forgot.
- One line: "Your check-ins stay in this browser. Use Settings, then Export, to back them up."
- If a yellow "isn''t saving" note shows where they opened it, they can download the file and open it in their browser instead.
No feature list, no recap of the code.
', '[{"input":"can you make me a habit tracker? I want to drink more water, read 20 minutes before bed and stretch in the morning. I like green","output":"The skill''s answer is a full web page. Open it: /projects/skill-quest/templates/habit-tracker/result-skill.html","plain_output":"Plain Claude''s answer is a full web page. Open it: /projects/skill-quest/templates/habit-tracker/result-plain.html","plain_source":"test","output_link":"/projects/skill-quest/templates/habit-tracker/result-skill.html","plain_link":"/projects/skill-quest/templates/habit-tracker/result-plain.html"}]', 'can you make me a habit tracker? I want to drink more water, read 20 minutes before bed and stretch in the morning. I like green', 'smez-system', 1, 'live', '{"skill_score":5,"plain_score":3,"winner":"skill","reason":"the skill is forgiving (return ticket, backfill past days), lets users add, edit and delete habits, offers export and import, and is built for a phone; plain Claude has hardcoded habits, no streak forgiveness, a month grid that cuts off on a phone screen, and its water cups conflict with the habit check-off.","tested_at":"2026-11-23"}', 5, 3, 1795366800000, 1795366800000, 1795366800000);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO skills (id, slug, name, one_line, category, sub, for_tag, works_in, skill_md, examples, example_inputs, maker_id, version, status, test, test_skill, test_plain, created_at, updated_at, live_at) VALUES ('one-page-site', 'one-page-site', 'My one-page website', 'A personal site built around your real work.', 'build', 'personal-site', 'creator', '["claude-ai","claude-code"]', '---
name: one-page-site
description: Makes a one-page personal website (portfolio, about-me page or link-in-bio) as a single ready-to-share HTML file that looks designed by a person, not generated. The page is built around the person''s real work, with a typeface and colors from their world, their own facts and numbers, and an obvious way to reach them. Use when someone says "make me a website", "personal website", "portfolio site", "a page to show my work", "about me page", "link in bio page", "one-page site", "site for my art / photos / music / writing / freelance work", or "I need somewhere to send people".
---

# My one-page website

Plain AI makes the same personal site every time: a centered hero with "Hi, I''m X" and two buttons, three icon cards for "services", a purple gradient, Inter, and copy like "Passionate creative crafting meaningful experiences". Nobody remembers it. Your page must look like a designer met this person and built it around what they actually make.

## 1. Get the facts (at most 3 questions, once)

You need three things. Ask only for what is missing, all in one short message, then wait:
1. Who you are and what you do (name, what you make or do, where, for how long).
2. Three things you''ve done or made (a name, a year, a number, who it was for, a link or photo if they have one).
3. How people should reach you (email, and any links: shop, Instagram, booking).

If they already gave most of it, or say "just make it", do not ask. Build with what you have. Never invent facts: a missing detail becomes a short, clearly marked slot like `[year]` inside the caption, listed in your reply.

## 2. Pick one look

Read `references/looks.md` and choose ONE direction from the "Pick it when" lines, based on what the person makes, not at random and not by default. Use its palette, fonts, layout and signature detail exactly. If their answers name colors from their world (a glaze, a band''s album, a uniform), swap the accent for that color only if it keeps the listed contrast. Say which look you picked and why in one line of your reply.

## 3. Plan the page around the work

The work is the page. Build in this order and add nothing else:
1. **Masthead.** Their name as the h1, then one line saying what they do in plain words with one specific (place, medium, years, a client). A small contact link sits in the top corner from the first screen. No two-button hero, no "Hi, I''m", no tagline that any other person could use.
2. **The work.** The three things, laid out the way the look says. Make one of them the lead (bigger, first). Each gets a real caption: what it is, when, and one concrete fact (a number, a place, who it was for, what happened). This section takes more space than everything else together.
3. **About.** 40 to 80 words, first person, their voice. Include one opinion or one thing they don''t do ("I don''t do logos." "No digital prints, only the real paper."). Facts from their answers only.
4. **Contact.** The email written out as visible text and as a `mailto:` link, a "Copy email" button, and their other links as plain text links with the site named ("Instagram, @handle"). One line saying what to get in touch about and any real timing they gave ("Commissions open from March").
5. **Footer.** One line: name, city, year. Nothing more.

Never add: a services grid, skills bars or percentages, a stats row, testimonials, client logos, a newsletter box, a blog teaser, "Let''s work together!" banners, a dark-mode toggle, or a nav menu (a one-page site with five short parts needs none; the corner contact link is enough).

**Link-in-bio** requests (mostly links, little work to show): use the Link card look and turn each link into a large row with the site name and one line on why to tap it. Same rules otherwise.

## 4. Pictures

- If they gave image links, use them with `alt` text that describes the actual piece ("Wide celadon bowl with a dark iron rim, about 30 cm across").
- If not, never use stock photos, AI-drawn people, or emoji, and never leave big empty boxes (a wall of blank frames looks unfinished). Fill each frame (fixed `aspect-ratio`, the look''s frame color) with a stand-in that carries information, and make the three stand-ins different from each other:
  - the lead piece: a simple drawn SVG of the real thing in the accent (a pot''s profile, a book''s spread, a floor plan, a waveform), one stroke weight, `aria-hidden="true"`, filling about 80% of the frame;
  - the others: their key number set huge in the display face and the accent ("120" with "plates, five months" under it), or a short real quote from the person.
  - no visible "photo coming" or "placeholder" text anywhere: the stand-in must look finished on its own, as if it were the intended design. Put only an HTML comment above each frame: `<!-- Swap in your photo: <img src="your-photo.jpg" alt="..."> -->`.
  Do not repeat the caption''s title inside the frame. The caption under it is still full and real.

## 5. Write the words

Read `references/copy.md` and follow it. The short version: their facts and numbers beat adjectives, one point of view, sentence case, short sentences, no AI words, almost no em dashes. Total page copy under 300 words.

## 6. Build it (exact output)

One complete HTML file, delivered as a single artifact or code block, nothing else needed to open it:
- `<!doctype html>`, `<html lang="en">`, `<meta charset="utf-8">`, `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">`, a `<title>` of "Name, what they do" and a one-sentence `<meta name="description">`.
- Fonts: the look''s Google Fonts `<link>` (only the weights you use, `display=swap`) with a fallback stack. No other external files, scripts, frameworks, icon fonts or images you made up.
- All CSS in one `<style>`, colors as custom properties named after the look''s roles (`--bg`, `--ink`, `--muted`, `--accent`, ...). Only use the text/background pairs listed in the look; they are already checked for 4.5:1 contrast.
- Semantic HTML: `header`, `main`, one `h1`, `section`s with `h2`s, `footer`. Real `<a href>` for links and `<button>` for the copy button. Tap targets at least 44 px tall.
- Spacing: the width wrapper sets only `padding-inline`, sections set only `padding-block` (a `padding: 56px 0` shorthand on a section silently wipes the side gutters). Put dividing rules on the section, not on a full-bleed element.
- Mobile first. At 375 px wide: no sideways scrolling, the name fits (use `clamp()` and `overflow-wrap: anywhere` on long names), work stacks in one column. From 760 px: the look''s desktop layout. Max content width as the look says.
- Craft details: links underlined with `text-underline-offset: .2em`, `:focus-visible` outline in the accent, `::selection` in the look''s colors, `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` on years and numbers, `text-wrap: balance` on headings.
- Motion: only the look''s one motion moment, inside `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)`. Content is visible without it.
- JS: only the copy-email button (under 15 lines, `navigator.clipboard.writeText`, then the button says "Copied" for 2 seconds; if clipboard fails, select the email text). Nothing else.

## 7. Hard rules

- Banned looks: centered hero with two buttons, three same-size icon cards, purple or indigo, gradients of any kind on text or backgrounds, glass or blur, glow, blobs, floating tilted mockups, hard offset shadows, every corner the same big radius.
- Banned type: Inter, Roboto, Arial, Helvetica, Poppins, Montserrat, Open Sans, Space Grotesk, system-ui as the display face.
- Banned labels: no small uppercase "eyebrow" text above headings, no 01/02/03 section numbers, no emoji anywhere.
- Banned content: invented clients, numbers, quotes, awards or logos; lorem ipsum; "[Your Name]" style placeholders left unmarked.

## 8. Self-check before you answer
- Breathing room: any box with a border, outline or colored background has at least 12px of space between its text and its edge (16px on desktop).

Look at the page as a designer would at 375 px and at desktop. Fix anything that fails, then check again:
1. Could another person paste the masthead line onto their own site unchanged? It must be no.
2. Is the first screen about this person''s work, not a generic welcome? On a 1280 x 800 screen the top of the lead piece must show.
3. Is the typeface the look''s pair, not a default? Is the palette the look''s, with no purple and no gradient?
4. Is there one clear lead piece, not equal cards?
5. Are there no glass, glow, blobs, eyebrow labels, emoji or section numbers?
6. Are all pictures real (theirs) or honest frames, with no stock or AI people?
7. Does every caption hold at least one real fact from their answers?
8. Can you find and copy the email in under 5 seconds on a phone?
9. Would a designer believe a person made this? If it looks like a template, change the layout of the work section first.

## 9. Your reply

Keep it short: the HTML file, then at most 4 lines: the look you picked and why; anything you marked as a slot to fill (like `[year]`); how to swap in photos ("search the file for `Swap in your photo` and replace the drawing with your image"); and that they can publish it as an artifact or upload the file to any host.
', '[{"input":"hi can you make me a website? just one page, something i can put on my instagram and send to shops. i''m Maren Okafor, i make stoneware pottery (mostly bowls and mugs) in my garage studio in West Asheville, NC. been doing it about 6 years, full time since 2023. i fire to cone 10 and mix my own glazes, my favorite is a pale green celadon.\n\n3 things i''m proud of:\n- my Tide bowls, a series of 40 bowls with a dark iron rim, they sold out in about 2 hours at the Asheville Fall Pottery Market in 2025\n- i made 120 dinner plates for Little Ostra, a seafood restaurant on Haywood Rd, in 2024. took me 5 months\n- i teach a beginner wheel class at Odd Clay co-op every tuesday night, 8 people max, i''ve taught around 150 people so far\n\ncontact: maren@okafor-clay.com, instagram @maren.makes.clay. i take commissions and wholesale orders but my next opening is March 2027. i don''t do custom pet portraits on mugs lol people keep asking","output":"The skill''s answer is a full web page. Open it: /projects/skill-quest/templates/one-page-site/result-skill.html","plain_output":"Plain Claude''s answer is a full web page. Open it: /projects/skill-quest/templates/one-page-site/result-plain.html","plain_source":"test","output_link":"/projects/skill-quest/templates/one-page-site/result-skill.html","plain_link":"/projects/skill-quest/templates/one-page-site/result-plain.html"}]', 'hi can you make me a website? just one page, something i can put on my instagram and send to shops. i''m Maren Okafor, i make stoneware pottery (mostly bowls and mugs) in my garage studio in West Asheville, NC. been doing it about 6 years, full time since 2023. i fire to cone 10 and mix my own glazes, my favorite is a pale green celadon.

3 things i''m proud of:
- my Tide bowls, a series of 40 bowls with a dark iron rim, they sold out in about 2 hours at the Asheville Fall Pottery Market in 2025
- i made 120 dinner plates for Little Ostra, a seafood restaurant on Haywood Rd, in 2024. took me 5 months
- i teach a beginner wheel class at Odd Clay co-op every tuesday night, 8 people max, i''ve taught around 150 people so far

contact: maren@okafor-clay.com, instagram @maren.makes.clay. i take commissions and wholesale orders but my next opening is March 2027. i don''t do custom pet portraits on mugs lol people keep asking', 'smez-system', 1, 'live', '{"skill_score":4.5,"plain_score":3.8,"winner":"skill","reason":"the skill sticks to Maren''s facts and looks custom-designed (kiln-shelf layout, celadon accent, copy-email button), while plain Claude is a generic centered template that invents details like a waitlist and ''custom bowls, mugs and sets, shops and restaurants welcome''.","tested_at":"2026-11-23"}', 4.5, 3.8, 1795366800000, 1795366800000, 1795366800000);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO skills (id, slug, name, one_line, category, sub, for_tag, works_in, skill_md, examples, example_inputs, maker_id, version, status, test, test_skill, test_plain, created_at, updated_at, live_at) VALUES ('trip-planner', 'trip-planner', 'Trip planner', 'A day-by-day trip plan you can actually follow.', 'travel', 'trips', 'anyone', '["claude-ai","claude-code"]', '---
name: trip-planner
description: Plans a realistic day-by-day trip as one phone-friendly page with Google Maps links, a backup for rain, rough costs in the local currency and a packing checklist you can tick. Use when someone says "plan my trip", "make an itinerary", "what should we do in [city]", "3 days in Rome", "weekend in Chicago with the kids", "honeymoon plan", or gives a destination and dates and wants a plan.
---

# Trip planner

Make a trip plan people can actually follow on their phone: few stops a day, close together, with honest "check before you go" notes instead of made-up facts. The result is one HTML page built from `assets/trip.html`.

## 1. Get the basics (ask at most 2 questions)

You need: where, how many days (or dates), who is going, budget or style, what they like.

- If the destination or the length is missing, ask for it. Ask nothing else unless it changes the plan a lot (for example, "Are kids coming?").
- Ask both questions in one message, then wait.
- For anything else, pick a default and list it in `assumptions`: 2 adults, mid budget, a central hotel, walking plus public transport, arriving the evening before day 1.

## 2. Plan the days (the rules that make this better)

- **Pace.** At most 3 stops a day marked `"kind": "anchor"`, and only 2 if kids, older travelers or a hot month are involved. Meals and the slow morning don''t count as anchors.
- **Slow morning.** Every day starts with an unhurried breakfast or coffee stop (`"kind": "slow"`). No sight before about 9:30.
- **One area per day.** Group each day''s stops in one neighborhood or two that touch. Name it in `area`. Don''t zigzag across the city.
- **Travel time.** Put a `{"move": "..."}` line between stops that are more than a few minutes apart: "15 min walk", "25 min by metro". Keep each move under about 30 minutes. A day trip out of town counts as the whole day.
- **First and last day are light.** Arrival day: one easy stop and dinner. Departure day: breakfast, one stop near the hotel, then leave time to get to the airport or station.
- **Rain plan.** Every day gets one indoor `rain` option in the same area.
- **Kids.** Add a rest or playground stop after lunch and keep moves short.
- **Free time.** Leave at least one afternoon or evening open on trips of 4 days or more ("Free afternoon: wander, nap, shop").
- **Match their taste.** If they said "no museums", use at most one, and only as a rain option.

## 3. Be honest about places

- Use well-known places you are confident exist: major sights, famous markets, named neighborhoods, well-known streets and parks. Never invent a restaurant, shop or venue. For meals, describe the type and area ("dinner in Trastevere, many trattorias") with a map search like `trattoria Trastevere Rome`.
- Never state exact opening hours, prices or that something is open on a given day as fact. When it matters (closed days, timed tickets, reservations, seasonal closures, religious dress codes), add a `check` line: "Often closed on Mondays. Check before you go." or "Timed tickets usually sell out. Book on the official site."
- `map` is a Google Maps search phrase: the place name plus the city, for example `Colosseum Rome`. Never write street addresses.
- Costs are ranges in the local currency (ISO code in `currency`, for example EUR, JPY, MXN). Cover lodging, food, local transport, tickets. They are rough estimates; say so in `costNote`. Don''t include flights unless asked.

## 4. Build the page

1. Read `assets/trip.html` in this skill''s folder.
2. Copy it exactly. Change only the JSON inside `<script type="application/json" id="trip">`. Don''t edit the CSS or JavaScript; they draw the day lines, map links, route links, cost totals, saved ticks and print layout.
3. Output the full file as one HTML artifact (in Claude.ai) or save it as `trip-plan.html` (in Claude Code or other file tools).
4. If you can''t read the asset, build one self-contained HTML page with the same sections, in the same order, following the same rules.

### Data fields

- `lang`: page language code ("en", "es"...). Write the plan in the language the user wrote in.
- `title`: short, like "Rome, 4 days with a 9 year old". `subtitle`: dates, who, budget.
- `currency`: ISO code of the destination''s currency.
- `assumptions`: 1 to 4 short lines with the defaults you picked.
- `days[]`: `label` ("Day 1"), `date` ("Tue 2 Jun", or leave "" if no dates), `area`, `mode` ("walking", "transit" or "driving", used for the route link), `getting` (how they move that day, one line), `stops[]`, `rain`.
- Each stop: `when` (Morning, Late morning, Lunch, Afternoon, Evening), `kind` ("slow", "anchor", "food" or "free"), `title`, `note` (1 or 2 short sentences: what to do and roughly how long), `map`, optional `check`.
- A move between stops: `{"move": "20 min walk"}`.
- `rain`: `title`, `note`, `map`, optional `check`.
- `warnings`: 3 to 6 trip-wide "check before you go" lines (bookings to make ahead, closing days, holidays, strikes or seasonal things to look up, local transport card, tipping or cash habits). Phrase each as something to check, not a fact.
- `costs[]`: `item`, `for` ("2 people, 4 nights"), `low`, `high` as plain numbers. The page adds the total.
- `costNote`: one sentence saying these are estimates.
- `checklist[]`: groups `{"group": "...", "items": [...]}`. Always "Before you go" (each booking from the plan, travel insurance, documents, bank card abroad, offline map) and "Pack" (fit the season, the walking, the plug type, anything for kids). 6 to 12 items per group.

Keep JSON valid: double quotes, no trailing commas, no line breaks inside strings, never the text `</script>`.

## 5. Write like a person

Short, plain sentences. No "vibrant", "hidden gem", "nestled", "bustling", "must-see", "iconic", "charming", "breathtaking", "immerse", "unforgettable". No em dashes. Say what they will do and see: "Walk the top deck of the bridge for the view", not "Experience the breathtaking views".

## 6. In the chat

Before the artifact: one line saying what you made and the defaults you assumed. After it: one line inviting a change ("Want a slower day, a day trip, or different food?"). Nothing else.

## 7. Check before you send

- [ ] No day has more than 3 anchors (2 with kids or older travelers), and every day has a slow morning, an area and a rain option.
- [ ] Moves are listed and each is under about 30 minutes.
- [ ] Day 1 and the last day are light.
- [ ] Every place is well known and real; meals are areas, not invented restaurants.
- [ ] No exact hours or prices stated as fact; risky things carry a `check` line.
- [ ] Every `map` is "place + city"; no street addresses.
- [ ] Costs are low/high numbers in the local currency, called estimates.
- [ ] The checklist includes every booking the plan relies on.
- [ ] The JSON is valid and the rest of the template is untouched.
- [ ] None of the banned words, no em dashes.
', '[{"input":"# Test request\n\nSample, made up for testing.\n\n> we''re going to rome for 4 days in early june, me, my husband and our 9 year old. mid budget. we like food and just walking around, not big on museums. our kid gets tired after lunch. can you plan it?","output":"The skill''s answer is a full web page. Open it: /projects/skill-quest/templates/trip-planner/result-skill.html","plain_output":"# Rome for 4 days in early June: family plan (2 adults + 9-year-old)\n\nPlan notes: early June is warm (about 27-30°C / 80-86°F) and busy. Your kid fades after lunch, so each day has **active mornings, a long lunch, then something easy** (gelato, a park, a fountain, a slow stroll). Museums are minimal. There is one optional, kid-friendly highlight.\n\n## Day 1: Historic center, easy start\n- **Morning:** Piazza Navona, then the Pantheon (quick look inside, about 20 min). Walk to Trevi Fountain. Go early, before 9:30, to beat the crowds.\n- **Lunch:** Pasta in a trattoria near the Pantheon, such as Armando al Pantheon (book ahead) or a casual spot on a side street.\n- **Afternoon (low energy):** Gelato at Giolitti or Fatamorgana. Stroll to the Spanish Steps and sit for a while.\n- **Dinner:** Early, around 7:30. Pizza al taglio or a casual pizzeria.\n\n## Day 2: Ancient Rome, done lightly\n- **Morning:** Colosseum exterior and Roman Forum views from the street and from Via dei Fori Imperiali. If you want to go inside, book timed tickets ahead. Kids often love the Colosseum, so this is the one \"sight\" worth considering.\n- **Lunch:** Monti neighborhood, a pretty, walkable area with good trattorias.\n- **Afternoon:** Wander Monti''s lanes and Piazza della Madonna dei Monti. Optional: Villa Borghese gardens (bike or rowboat rental) if energy is left.\n- **Dinner:** Cacio e pepe or carbonara in Monti.\n\n## Day 3: Trastevere and the Gianicolo\n- **Morning:** Campo de'' Fiori market, then cross to the Jewish Ghetto for fried artichokes (carciofi alla giudia) and pastries at Boccione.\n- **Lunch:** Long lunch in the Ghetto.\n- **Afternoon:** Slow walk across the Tiber Island to Trastevere. Rest at a cafe, then wander the lanes.\n- **Late afternoon:** Gianicolo Hill for sunset views. Take a taxi up if tired.\n- **Dinner:** Trastevere, which is lively and good for kids. Book ahead.\n\n## Day 4: Vatican area, flexible\n- **Morning:** St. Peter''s Square and Basilica. Go early, since the dress code requires covered shoulders and knees. Skip the Vatican Museums. Optionally climb the dome (about 550 steps, so check your kid''s stamina).\n- **Lunch:** Prati neighborhood, which has less touristy, better-value food than near the Vatican. Try Pizzarium (Bonci) for famous pizza al taglio.\n- **Afternoon:** Castel Sant''Angelo and a walk along the Tiber, or a free afternoon for souvenirs and gelato.\n- **Farewell dinner:** Pick a favorite from the trip, or try a Roman classic such as supplì, amatriciana or saltimbocca.\n\n## Practical tips\n- **Heat:** Start by 8:30-9:00. Rest 1-4 pm if needed. Bring water. Refill at the free *nasoni* drinking fountains.\n- **Kid energy:** Build in a hotel break after lunch on any day when your kid is wiped out. Rome is walkable, but use taxis or the app FreeNow for tired legs.\n- **Reservations:** Book popular dinners and any Colosseum or dome tickets in advance.\n- **Dining times:** Romans eat dinner late. Many restaurants open at 7:00-7:30 pm, which suits kids better.\n- **Where to stay:** Centro Storico, Monti, or Trastevere, for walkability.\n- **Pickpockets:** Be careful around Termini, the Colosseum and crowded sites.\n\n## Budget (mid-range, rough per day for 3 people)\n- Food: about €100-150\n- Sights and tickets: €20-60\n- Local transport and taxis: €10-30\n\nWant me to adjust the pace, add a day trip such as Ostia Antica (which kids often like), or suggest specific hotels?","plain_source":"test","output_link":"/projects/skill-quest/templates/trip-planner/result-skill.html"}]', '# Test request

Sample, made up for testing.

> we''re going to rome for 4 days in early june, me, my husband and our 9 year old. mid budget. we like food and just walking around, not big on museums. our kid gets tired after lunch. can you plan it?', 'smez-system', 1, 'live', '{"skill_score":4.5,"plain_score":3.5,"winner":"skill","reason":"the skill paces each day around the post-lunch rest, flags what to check, and adds a rain backup, costs, a checklist and map links, while plain Claude names more real restaurants but packs the days (Day 1 morning, Day 3 sunset, a dome climb) and treats the rest as optional.","tested_at":"2026-11-23"}', 4.5, 3.5, 1795366800000, 1795366800000, 1795366800000);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO skills (id, slug, name, one_line, category, sub, for_tag, works_in, skill_md, examples, example_inputs, maker_id, version, status, test, test_skill, test_plain, created_at, updated_at, live_at) VALUES ('meal-plan', 'meal-plan', 'Meal plan + grocery list', 'A week of dinners and one list to shop.', 'home', 'meals', 'parent', '["claude-ai","claude-code"]', '---
name: meal-plan
description: Makes a week of dinners (and lunches if asked) plus one merged grocery list grouped by store aisle, as a phone-friendly page with tick boxes. Reuses ingredients to cut waste and cost, plans leftovers, keeps weeknights to 30 minutes and follows every allergy and diet strictly. Use when someone says "meal plan", "what should we eat this week", "dinner ideas for the week", "grocery list", "shopping list for meals", "cheap family dinners", "meal prep", or lists who they feed, a budget and food limits.
---

# Meal plan + grocery list

Make a week of meals a tired person can actually cook, and a grocery list they can shop from in one pass. The result is one HTML page built from `assets/meal-plan.html`.

## 1. Get the basics (ask at most 2 questions)

You need: how many people (and ages of kids), allergies and diet limits, budget, time on weeknights, what they like or hate.

- Allergies are the one thing you never guess. If they mention kids or a family but say nothing about allergies, ask: "Any allergies or foods to avoid?" If they give a budget and people, ask nothing else.
- Ask at most 2 questions in one message, then wait.
- Otherwise use these defaults and list them in `assumptions`: 7 dinners, no lunches, 2 adults, a mid budget for their country, 30 minutes on weeknights, one longer cook on Sunday, a normal supermarket, the user''s local currency and units.

## 2. Plan the week (the rules that make this better)

- **Allergies and diets are absolute.** Not one ingredient, garnish, sauce or swap may break them. Watch the hidden ones: peanut in satay, some curry pastes and baked goods; tree nuts in pesto; gluten in soy sauce, stock cubes and breadcrumbs; dairy in bread and margarine; fish in Worcestershire and Caesar dressing; gelatin and rennet for vegetarians. List each rule in `safety.rules` in plain words ("No peanuts or peanut oil in any recipe") and list the store-bought items that need a label check in `safety.labels`.
- **Reuse ingredients.** Build the week around 3 to 5 core ingredients that each appear in 2 or more meals (a pack of chicken thighs over two dinners, one bunch of cilantro in three). Avoid buying an item for a single small use; if you must, use the rest elsewhere.
- **Cook once, eat twice.** At least 2 meals make extra on purpose (`"type": "double"`, with a `note` saying where the extra goes) and later meals use it (`"type": "leftover"`, 10 minutes or less). A leftover can be the same dish or a new one ("Sunday''s roast chicken in Tuesday''s tacos"). Keep cooked leftovers within 3 days and say to reheat until hot all the way through.
- **Time.** Monday to Friday meals take 30 minutes or less, start to table. One weekend meal can take longer. Use the Sunday prep to cut weeknight time.
- **Taste and family.** Use what they like at least 4 times in the week. If kids are named, keep heat mild with a spicy option for adults on the side.
- **Budget.** Estimate each grocery item''s price for where they live, in their currency. The total must fit the budget. If it can''t, say so and cut cost (beans, eggs, frozen veg, cheaper cuts) rather than pretending.
- **Fresh food first.** Use the most perishable things (fish, soft herbs, salad leaves) early in the week and the sturdy ones (roots, cabbage, canned and frozen) later.
- Use common dishes and real cooking methods. Keep each recipe to 3 to 6 short steps.

## 3. No health claims

Don''t say a meal is healthy, balanced, high in protein, good for weight loss, or anything about calories or nutrients, unless the user asks; even then, give no medical advice. The page already says it is a cooking plan, not medical advice. For a serious allergy, the page tells them to read labels; don''t promise anything is "safe".

## 4. Build the page

1. Read `assets/meal-plan.html` in this skill''s folder.
2. Copy it exactly. Change only the JSON inside `<script type="application/json" id="plan">`. Don''t edit the CSS or JavaScript; they build the week view, the "buy once, use all week" strip, saved ticks, budget total, the copy-list button and print layout.
3. Output the full file as one HTML artifact (in Claude.ai) or save it as `meal-plan.html` (in Claude Code or other file tools).
4. If you can''t read the asset, build one self-contained HTML page with the same sections, in the same order, following the same rules.

### Data fields

- `lang`, `title` (like "Dinners for 4, peanut-free"), `subtitle` (people, diet, budget, time), `currency` (ISO code), `budget` (number or null), `assumptions` (1 to 4 lines).
- `safety.rules`: one line per allergy or diet. `safety.labels`: store-bought items in this plan to check (tortillas, stock, sauces, bread, chocolate). If there are no limits, write one rule: "No allergies or diets given."
- `meals[]` in eating order: `day` ("Mon"), `meal` ("Dinner" or "Lunch"), `name`, `minutes` (total, start to table), `serves`, `type` ("cook", "double" or "leftover"), optional `note`, `uses`, `ingredients`, `steps`.
- `uses`: the main grocery items in this meal, spelled exactly as in `groceries` (the page matches them to show reuse). Skip pantry items.
- `ingredients`: amounts for this meal ("600 g chicken thighs"). `steps`: short, with times.
- `prep[]`: `task`, `minutes`. 3 to 6 Sunday tasks totaling 60 to 90 minutes (cook grains, chop onions, marinate, make a sauce, wash greens). Each task must save time on a named weeknight.
- `groceries[]`: `aisle` and `items` with `name`, `qty` (a buyable amount: "2 cans", "1 kg pack", "1 bunch"), `est` (number). One line per item for the whole week, quantities added up. Aisles in store-walk order: Produce, Meat and fish, Dairy and eggs, Bakery, Canned and dry, Spices and sauces, Frozen, Other.
- `pantry`: staples the recipes need that most homes have (oil, salt, pepper, flour, sugar, common dried spices, soy sauce). These are not on the grocery list.
- `estNote`: one sentence saying prices are estimates.

Keep JSON valid: double quotes, no trailing commas, no line breaks inside strings, never the text `</script>`.

## 5. Write like a person

Short, plain words. No "delicious", "vibrant", "flavor-packed", "wholesome", "guilt-free", "elevate", "hearty" or "family favorite". No em dashes. Dish names say what it is: "Chicken and black bean tacos", not "Fiesta Taco Night".

## 6. In the chat

Before the artifact: one line saying what you made and any default you assumed, plus the allergy rule you followed ("Every recipe is peanut-free"). After it: one line inviting a swap ("Want to swap a meal or change the budget?"). Nothing else.

## 7. Check before you send

- [ ] Re-read every ingredient, sauce and garnish against each allergy and diet. Zero exceptions.
- [ ] Every risky store-bought item is in `safety.labels`.
- [ ] At least 3 grocery items are used in 2 or more meals, and `uses` names match `groceries` exactly.
- [ ] At least 2 "double" meals, each feeding a later "leftover" meal within 3 days.
- [ ] Every Monday to Friday meal is 30 minutes or less.
- [ ] The grocery list covers every ingredient not in `pantry`, merged with totals, and the estimate fits the budget (or the chat says it doesn''t).
- [ ] No health, nutrition or calorie claims.
- [ ] The JSON is valid and the rest of the template is untouched.
- [ ] None of the banned words, no em dashes.
', '[{"input":"# Test request\n\nSample, made up for testing.\n\n> can you make us a meal plan for the week? family of 4, me, my husband and 2 kids (7 and 11). the 7 year old is allergic to peanuts. budget is about $120 for the week. we like mexican food and pasta, nothing too spicy for the kids. weeknights i only have like 30 min to cook","output":"The skill''s answer is a full web page. Open it: /projects/skill-quest/templates/meal-plan/result-skill.html","plain_output":"# Weekly Meal Plan: Family of 4, Peanut-Free, About $120\n\nMild flavors, 30 minutes or less on weeknights, Mexican and pasta favorites.\n\n**Peanut safety:** Check labels on everything, especially tortillas, sauces, granola bars, and anything \"made in a facility with peanuts\". Skip peanut oil. Use olive or vegetable oil.\n\n## The Week\n\n| Day | Dinner | Time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mon | Chicken quesadillas with corn and black beans, side of salsa and sliced cucumber | 25 min |\n| Tue | Spaghetti with meat sauce and a green salad | 30 min |\n| Wed | Beef taco night (mild seasoning), with lettuce, cheese, tomatoes | 25 min |\n| Thu | Creamy chicken and broccoli penne | 30 min |\n| Fri | Sheet-pan chicken fajitas (peppers and onions, mild seasoning) with rice | 30 min |\n| Sat | Homemade cheese and veggie pizza night, with the kids helping | 40 min |\n| Sun | Slow cooker chicken tortilla soup, with toppings and leftover rice | 15 min prep |\n\n## Breakfasts (rotate)\n- Scrambled eggs and toast with fruit\n- Oatmeal with banana and cinnamon\n- Yogurt with berries (use a peanut-free granola)\n\n## Lunches\n- Turkey and cheese wraps with carrot sticks and apple slices\n- Leftover quesadilla, pasta, or soup, reheated\n\n## Snacks\n- Fruit, string cheese, popcorn, tortilla chips with mild salsa, cucumbers and hummus (check the label)\n\n## Grocery List (estimated about $115 to $120)\n\n**Produce (about $26)**\n- Bell peppers x4, onions x3, garlic x1 head\n- Broccoli x2 crowns, cucumbers x3, carrots 2 lb\n- Lettuce x2, tomatoes x5, limes x2\n- Bananas x1 bunch, apples x6, berries x1 pack (frozen is cheaper)\n- Avocado x2 (optional)\n\n**Meat (about $34)**\n- Chicken breasts or thighs, about 4 lb\n- Ground beef, 2 lb (use half for tacos, half for the spaghetti sauce)\n- Deli turkey, 1 lb\n\n**Dairy and eggs (about $18)**\n- Shredded Mexican cheese 2 bags, mozzarella 1 bag, string cheese\n- Eggs x1 dozen, milk x1 gallon, heavy cream or cream cheese (for the penne)\n- Yogurt, large tub\n\n**Pantry (about $32)**\n- Spaghetti 1 lb, penne 1 lb, rice 2 lb\n- Flour tortillas x2 packs, taco shells x1 box\n- Black beans x2 cans, corn x2 cans (or frozen)\n- Pasta sauce x1 jar, canned diced tomatoes x2, chicken broth x2 cartons\n- Mild salsa, pizza dough (or flour and yeast), pizza sauce\n- Oatmeal, bread, popcorn kernels, tortilla chips\n- Spices: chili powder, cumin, paprika, garlic powder, Italian seasoning (most are likely already in your cupboard)\n\n**Tip:** Buy store-brand items and check your pantry first. That can bring the total under $110.\n\n## Time-Savers for Weeknights\n- **Sunday:** Chop peppers, onions, and broccoli. Cook the rice for the week. Brown the beef for Tuesday and Wednesday in one batch.\n- **Slow cooker or Instant Pot:** Sunday soup lets you make extra chicken for Monday''s quesadillas.\n- **Shred the cheese yourself** when you have time. It''s cheaper than bagged.\n- Let the 11-year-old run the taco bar. Let the 7-year-old build pizzas.\n\n## Mild-Spice Notes\n- Use chili powder and cumin for flavor, not heat. Skip chipotle, jalapeno, and cayenne.\n- Put hot sauce and jalapenos on the table for the adults only.\n\n## Swaps\n- **Cheaper:** Use ground turkey or beans for tacos, or skip the avocado.\n- **More veggies:** Add spinach to the meat sauce and zucchini to the fajitas.\n- **Leftovers:** Pasta and soup both keep well for lunches.\n\nWould you like recipes with exact amounts for any of these, or a version that is vegetarian on two nights to cut the cost further?","plain_source":"test","output_link":"/projects/skill-quest/templates/meal-plan/result-skill.html"}]', '# Test request

Sample, made up for testing.

> can you make us a meal plan for the week? family of 4, me, my husband and 2 kids (7 and 11). the 7 year old is allergic to peanuts. budget is about $120 for the week. we like mexican food and pasta, nothing too spicy for the kids. weeknights i only have like 30 min to cook', 'smez-system', 1, 'live', '{"skill_score":4.8,"plain_score":3.5,"winner":"skill","reason":"the skill gives cookable recipes with steps, cook-once-eat-twice planning, a quantified merged grocery list (about $87) that matches the recipes, and peanut-label checks tied to specific products, while plain Claude is a vaguer outline with loose quantities and no recipes, though it does add breakfasts, lunches and snacks.","tested_at":"2026-11-23"}', 4.8, 3.5, 1795366800000, 1795366800000, 1795366800000);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO skills (id, slug, name, one_line, category, sub, for_tag, works_in, skill_md, examples, example_inputs, maker_id, version, status, test, test_skill, test_plain, created_at, updated_at, live_at) VALUES ('resume-tailor', 'resume-tailor', 'Resume tailor', 'Your resume fitted to one job, plus cover letter.', 'work', 'job-hunt', 'job-seeker', '["claude-ai","claude-code"]', '---
name: resume-tailor
description: Tailor a resume or CV to one specific job post and write a short cover letter, using only what is true in the person''s resume. Returns a match table against the job''s must-haves, a rewritten plain-text resume that applicant tracking systems can read, a list of things to confirm, keyword coverage and a cover letter under 200 words. Use when someone pastes a job post and their resume and says things like "tailor my resume", "update my CV for this job", "make my resume fit this posting", "help me apply for this job", "write me a cover letter for this", or "am I a good fit for this role". Not for building a resume from scratch with no work history.
---

# Resume tailor

The person wants this job. Your job is to show their real experience in the light this employer cares about. Plain AI tailoring tends to fail in the same ways: it invents numbers and achievements, stuffs in every keyword from the post (including skills the person does not have), and writes a cover letter full of "passionate" and "I am writing to express my interest". Recruiters spot all three. Do the opposite.

## Before you start

You need two things: the job post and their resume. If one is missing, ask for it and stop.

If both are there, do not ask questions. Assume:
- Keep their name, contact details, employers, job titles and dates exactly as written.
- One page if they have under 10 years of experience, two pages at most otherwise.
- Match their spelling and style: "CV" and UK spelling if they wrote that way, "resume" and US spelling if not.

Say these assumptions in one line at the top of your answer.

## Step 1: Read the job post

List the must-haves (stated as required, "you have", "must", or repeated) and the nice-to-haves (preferred, "bonus", "ideally"). Keep the employer''s exact words for each. Note one specific thing about this employer from the post itself (a project, who they serve, a recent change, how the team works) for the cover letter.

## Step 2: Match, honestly

For each must-have, find the evidence in their resume. Rate it:
- **Strong:** the resume clearly shows it.
- **Partial:** related experience that transfers (say how).
- **Gap:** nothing in the resume shows it.

A gap stays a gap. Do not cover it with vague wording. Tell them how to handle it (learn it, mention a related skill, or address it in the interview).

## Step 3: Rewrite the resume

- **Facts are fixed.** You may reorder, reword, merge, cut and move things to the top. You may not add any employer, title, date, degree, tool, number, team size, result or responsibility that is not in their resume.
- **Result first.** Start each bullet with the outcome if the resume gives one, then how. If the resume gives no result, start with a strong plain verb and the real scope (who, how many, how often) that is in the resume. Do not make up a result to fill the slot.
- **No invented metrics.** If a bullet would be much stronger with a number they did not give, leave the number out of the resume and add a question to "Please confirm" (for example: "About how many orders a day did you handle?").
- **Use the employer''s words where they are true.** If the post says "vendor management" and the resume says "dealt with suppliers", use "vendor management". Never add a keyword for a skill they do not show.
- **Order by this job.** Put the most relevant role and bullets first within each section. Cut bullets that do nothing for this job. Add a 2-line summary at the top that names the role and their real strongest match.
- **ATS-safe format.** Plain text. Standard headings: Summary, Experience, Skills, Education (plus Certifications or Volunteering if they have them). No tables, columns, icons, graphics, photos or text boxes in the resume. Dates in one consistent format. Bullets with "-".

## Step 4: Cover letter

- Under 200 words. Three short paragraphs.
- Open with the role and the one specific thing about this employer from the post, in plain words. If the post gives nothing specific, write `[one thing you like about this company]` and flag it.
- Middle: two real matches from the resume, each tied to a must-have.
- Close with one line asking for a conversation. Sign with their name.
- Never: "I am writing to express my interest", "passionate", "I believe I would be a great fit", "dynamic", "fast-paced", "team player", "leverage", "hit the ground running", em dashes.

## Output format

Use exactly these sections, in this order:

```
<one line: assumptions>

## How you match
| What they want | Your evidence | Match |
|---|---|---|
| <must-have, their words> | <short fact from your resume, or "Not in your resume"> | Strong / Partial / Gap |
(must-haves first, then up to 3 nice-to-haves)

**Your strongest angle:** <one sentence>
**Gaps to plan for:** <one line each: the gap and what to do about it, or "None">

## Your tailored resume
<plain text resume, ready to paste into a document>

## Please confirm
- <every reworded line that might overstate, and every number worth adding, as a short question>

## Keywords from the post
- Used: <keywords now in the resume>
- Left out (not in your resume): <keywords you did not add, so they can add them only if true>

## Cover letter
<letter>

(<word count> words)
```

## Self-check before you answer

Go through the tailored resume line by line and fix anything that fails:
- Every employer, title, date, tool, number and result appears in their original resume. Nothing new.
- No keyword appears for a skill they do not show. Gaps are marked Gap in the table, not hidden.
- Every bullet starts with a result or a strong verb plus real scope.
- No tables, columns or icons inside the resume section.
- Cover letter is under 200 words, names one specific thing from the post, and has none of the banned phrases.
- Nothing in brackets is left unflagged.
', '[{"input":"hi can you tailor my resume to this job and write a cover letter? i really want this one. ive been in retail for years and want to get out. resume and job below\n\nJOB POST:\n\nOperations Coordinator, Fernbrook Community Health (Riverside, full time, $52,000 to $58,000)\n\nFernbrook runs three community clinics and, starting in March, a mobile health van that will visit six neighborhoods a week. We need an Operations Coordinator to keep the clinics and the van stocked, staffed and on schedule.\n\nWhat you''ll do:\n- Build and manage weekly schedules for 25+ front desk and support staff\n- Track supply inventory across sites and place orders with vendors\n- Plan the mobile van''s routes and weekly calendar with the outreach team\n- Be the first point of contact when something goes wrong on a shift\n- Keep simple reports on supply costs and staffing hours\n\nYou have:\n- 2+ years scheduling staff or coordinating operations\n- Experience managing inventory and vendor orders\n- Strong Excel or Google Sheets skills\n- Calm, clear communication with staff and the public\n- Experience with Salesforce or a similar CRM\n\nNice to have:\n- Healthcare or nonprofit experience\n- Spanish\n- A driver''s license\n\nRESUME:\n\nDana Okafor\nRiverside | dana.okafor.sample@example.com | (555) 014-2290\n\nExperience\n\nStore Shift Manager, Hollis Home Goods, Riverside, Jun 2021 to present\n- responsible for making the weekly schedule for about 30 staff\n- did the stock counts every week and ordered from our 8 main suppliers\n- handled customer complaints and problems on shift\n- trained new hires\n- made a google sheet to track overtime which cut overtime hours a lot\n- opened and closed the store\n\nSales Associate, Hollis Home Goods, Riverside, Mar 2019 to Jun 2021\n- helped customers, ran the register\n- did visual merchandising\n- employee of the month twice\n\nVolunteer, Eastside Food Pantry, 2020 to present\n- sort donations saturdays, helped set up the pop up pantry route to 4 apartment buildings\n\nSkills: Excel, Google Sheets, Square POS, scheduling, customer service, conversational Spanish\n\nEducation\nRiverside Community College, Associate in Business, 2019","output":"Assumptions: US spelling and \"resume\", one page (under 10 years of experience), and your name, contact details, employers, titles and dates kept exactly as written.\n\n## How you match\n| What they want | Your evidence | Match |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2+ years scheduling staff or coordinating operations | Made the weekly schedule for about 30 staff as Store Shift Manager since Jun 2021 | Strong |\n| Experience managing inventory and vendor orders | Weekly stock counts and orders from 8 main suppliers (one store, not multiple sites) | Strong |\n| Strong Excel or Google Sheets skills | Built a Google Sheet to track overtime; Excel and Google Sheets in your skills | Strong |\n| Calm, clear communication with staff and the public | Handled customer complaints and problems on shift; trained new hires; customer service | Strong |\n| Experience with Salesforce or a similar CRM | Not in your resume (Square POS is a register system, not a CRM) | Gap |\n| Healthcare or nonprofit experience | Volunteer at Eastside Food Pantry since 2020 (nonprofit, not healthcare) | Partial |\n| Spanish | Conversational Spanish in your skills | Partial |\n| A driver''s license | Not in your resume | Gap |\n\n**Your strongest angle:** You already schedule about 30 staff, run weekly stock counts and supplier orders, and handle problems on shift, which is most of this job, and you helped set up a pantry route to 4 buildings, which is close to planning the van''s routes.\n\n**Gaps to plan for:**\n- CRM (Salesforce or similar): Not in your resume. If you have used any customer database or tracking tool, tell me and I will add it. Otherwise say in the interview that you build your own trackers in Google Sheets and learn tools quickly, and consider a free Salesforce Trailhead course before you apply.\n- Driver''s license: Not in your resume. It is only a nice-to-have, but the role involves a van. If you have one, add it. If not, be ready to say how you would handle that.\n\n## Your tailored resume\n\nDana Okafor\nRiverside | dana.okafor.sample@example.com | (555) 014-2290\n\nSummary\nOperations-minded shift manager who makes the weekly schedule for about 30 staff, runs weekly inventory counts and orders from 8 main suppliers. Experienced in tracking data in Google Sheets and handling problems on shift, with community pantry route experience.\n\nExperience\n\nStore Shift Manager, Hollis Home Goods, Riverside, Jun 2021 to present\n- Cut overtime hours by building a Google Sheet to track overtime\n- Made the weekly schedule for about 30 staff\n- Counted stock every week and ordered from our 8 main suppliers (vendor orders)\n- Handled customer complaints and problems on shift as the point of contact\n- Trained new hires\n- Opened and closed the store\n\nSales Associate, Hollis Home Goods, Riverside, Mar 2019 to Jun 2021\n- Named employee of the month twice\n- Helped customers and ran the register\n- Did visual merchandising\n\nVolunteering\n\nVolunteer, Eastside Food Pantry, 2020 to present\n- Helped set up the pop up pantry route to 4 apartment buildings\n- Sort donations on Saturdays\n\nSkills\nExcel, Google Sheets, Square POS, scheduling, customer service, conversational Spanish\n\nEducation\nRiverside Community College, Associate in Business, 2019\n\n## Please confirm\n- About how much did your overtime tracker cut overtime hours (a percent, or hours per week or month)? Your resume says \"a lot\", so I left the number out.\n- Is \"point of contact\" fair for how you handle problems on shift (are you the person staff go to first)? Change it if not.\n- \"Vendor orders\": are your 8 main suppliers vendors in the normal sense (companies you place orders with)? Confirm that wording is right.\n- Did your ordering cover more than one location or just the one store? Say so if it did.\n- About how many items or how much money in stock did you count and order each week?\n- Has your overtime tracker or schedule been used to report on labor costs or hours? Only say yes if true (the post wants simple reports on costs and staffing hours).\n- Do you have a driver''s license? And have you used any CRM or customer database, even a simple one?\n- How many people did you train as shift manager?\n- How often does the pantry route run, and roughly how many households does it reach?\n\n## Keywords from the post\n- Used: scheduling, schedules, staff, inventory (as stock counts), vendor orders, Excel, Google Sheets, customer complaints, problems on shift, nonprofit (via volunteering), Spanish, route\n- Left out (not in your resume): Salesforce, CRM, healthcare, driver''s license, supply costs reports, staffing hours reports. Add them only if true.\n\n## Cover letter\nDear Hiring Team,\n\nI am applying for the Operations Coordinator role at Fernbrook Community Health. A mobile health van visiting six neighborhoods a week is a big step for a clinic network, and I would like to help keep it stocked and on schedule.\n\nAt Hollis Home Goods I have made the weekly schedule for about 30 staff since June 2021. I also do the stock counts every week, order from our 8 main suppliers, and handle problems on shift. A Google Sheet I built to track overtime cut overtime hours.\n\nI also volunteer at Eastside Food Pantry, where I helped set up the pop up pantry route to 4 apartment buildings. I speak conversational Spanish. I would welcome a conversation about the role.\n\nSincerely,\nDana Okafor\n\n(126 words)","plain_output":"Retail to operations is a very doable move, and your resume already has most of what this job asks for. Below are the tailored resume and a cover letter. I only used facts from your resume. Items marked [CONFIRM] are numbers or details I''d like you to check before sending.\n\n## Tailored resume\n\n**Dana Okafor**\nRiverside | dana.okafor.sample@example.com | (555) 014-2290\n\n**Summary**\nOperations-minded shift manager with 4+ years scheduling a team of about 30, managing inventory and vendor orders, and solving problems on shift. Strong in Excel and Google Sheets. Conversational Spanish. Volunteer with a local food pantry.\n\n**Experience**\n\n**Store Shift Manager**, Hollis Home Goods, Riverside, Jun 2021 to present\n- Build and manage the weekly schedule for about 30 staff\n- Run weekly stock counts and place orders with 8 main suppliers\n- First point of contact for customer complaints and problems on shift, resolved calmly and quickly\n- Built a Google Sheet to track overtime, which cut overtime hours [CONFIRM: add a number or percentage if you have one]\n- Trained new hires\n- Opened and closed the store, responsible for daily readiness\n\n**Sales Associate**, Hollis Home Goods, Riverside, Mar 2019 to Jun 2021\n- Served customers and ran the register\n- Handled visual merchandising\n- Named Employee of the Month twice\n\n**Volunteer**, Eastside Food Pantry, 2020 to present\n- Sort donations every Saturday\n- Helped set up the pop-up pantry route serving 4 apartment buildings\n\n**Skills**\nExcel, Google Sheets, scheduling, inventory and vendor ordering, customer service, conversational Spanish, Square POS\n\n**Education**\nAssociate in Business, Riverside Community College, 2019\n\n## Cover letter\n\nDear Hiring Team at Fernbrook Community Health,\n\nI''m applying for the Operations Coordinator role. For the past four years I have kept a busy store running by scheduling about 30 people, counting and ordering stock, and handling whatever went wrong on a shift. I want to do that work for a team that serves the community, and Fernbrook''s new mobile health van is exactly the kind of project I''d like to help launch.\n\nYour posting asks for scheduling, inventory, and spreadsheets. Here is what I''ve done:\n\n- **Scheduling:** I build the weekly schedule for about 30 staff at Hollis Home Goods, close to the 25+ you describe.\n- **Inventory and vendors:** I do the weekly stock counts and place orders with our 8 main suppliers.\n- **Spreadsheets and reporting:** I built a Google Sheet to track overtime, which cut overtime hours. The same habit would work for your supply cost and staffing hours reports.\n- **When things go wrong:** As shift manager I''m the person people turn to with a problem, from an upset customer to a short-staffed shift. I stay calm and clear.\n\nRoute planning is also familiar to me. At Eastside Food Pantry I volunteer on Saturdays and helped set up the pop-up pantry route to four apartment buildings. I know how much work it takes to get supplies and people to the right place on time, and how much it means to the neighbors on the other end. I also speak conversational Spanish.\n\nI haven''t worked in healthcare, and I''d be learning your systems, including a CRM, from the start. I pick up new tools quickly, and I''m ready to put in the work.\n\nI''d welcome the chance to talk. Thank you for your time.\n\nSincerely,\nDana Okafor\n(555) 014-2290 | dana.okafor.sample@example.com\n\n## Notes before you send\n\n1. **Salesforce/CRM:** Your resume doesn''t mention one. I didn''t claim it. If you''ve used any customer database or loyalty system at Hollis, add it. The letter is honest about this gap.\n2. **Driver''s license:** The job lists it as nice to have, and your resume is silent. If you have one, add \"valid driver''s license\" to your skills. It matters for a van role.\n3. **Overtime result:** \"Cut a lot\" is vague. Even a rough number helps, like \"cut overtime about 20%\". Only use a real figure.\n4. **Years of experience:** The job wants 2+ years scheduling. You have about 4 and a half as shift manager (Jun 2021 to now). I wrote \"4+ years\" in the summary.\n5. **Spanish:** I kept it as \"conversational\" to stay accurate.\n6. **Title:** Keep your real title. The operations language is in the bullets.\n7. **Length:** The resume fits on one page.\n\nIf you tell me the details for notes 1 to 3, I''ll update both documents.","plain_source":"test"}]', 'hi can you tailor my resume to this job and write a cover letter? i really want this one. ive been in retail for years and want to get out. resume and job below

